  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Turning warmer and more humid with a return of shower and\nthunderstorm chances for Thursday and Friday.\n\nA ridge of high pressure, both at the surface and aloft, is\ncontinuing to provide sunny skies this afternoon, with\ntemperatures heading well into the 80s for most areas. This \nwill lead into a mostly clear start to the night, but cloudiness\nis expected to increase after midnight from the north and west \n(first encroaching on the Alleghenies).\n\nFor Thursday, the prospects for showers and thunderstorms will\nincrease by mid to late afternoon, especially for the \nAlleghenies, the Shenandoah Valley, and the metroplex. This is\ntied to a slow moving cold front, making a gradual approach from\nthe Ohio Valley and Pennsylvania. As you head farther southeast\nthrough Virginia and into the coastal plain, the chances for\nrain should decrease. Thursday looks like the warmest day of the\nweek, although this is contingent on how much sunshine/heating\ncan take place earlier in the day. At this juncture, we're\nexpecting highs well into the 80s again, with parts of the\nShenandoah Valley up towards the metroplex perhaps making a run\nat 90.\n\nBy Friday, the frontal boundary itself should be across central\nto southern VA. This will lead to a continued chance of showers,\nalong with somewhat cooler readings as compared to Thursday.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain-free and less muggy conditions are likely on Saturday,\nbefore the weather turns more unsettled again from Sunday into \nnext week.\n\nCurrent indications point to a rain-free start to the weekend,\nwith the above mentioned cold front pushing down into the\nCarolinas, and weak high pressure building down from the north.\n\nHowever, signals also point to more unsettled weather from\nSunday into next week, as a persistent upper ridge axis gets \nshunted towards the deep South, allowing upper-level impulses \nto move across the Mid-Atlantic from the west. This should\nprovide the impetus for at least hit and miss showers and\nthunderstorms on a daily basis.",
